<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euric</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/484">484</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euric was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> from 466 to 484, having taken the throne by murdering his brother Theodoric II. Breaking with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he extended Visigothic rule over most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and, in the 470s, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis, ending Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. His law code, the Code of Euric, is the oldest surviving Visigothic legislation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euric.jpg" alt="Euric" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Rodríguez de Guzmán · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurico,_rey_de_los_Visigodos_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Euric</b>, king from 466, broke openly with Rome: his commanders captured Pamplona and Caesaraugusta, then Tarraco and the coastal cities, probably around 472–475.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Euric</b>'s annexations and above all after Vouillé, Goths settled more freely under their clan rulers, the reiks, who received dukedoms or comital offices, around Mérida, Barcelona, Seville, Toledo and Narbonne, the bases of Gothic power, and in the farming country between the upper Douro, Ebro and Tagus,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At Toulouse, Alaric II, great-grandson of Alaric I, who sacked Rome in 410, succeeded his father <b>Euric</b> as king of the Visigoths.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-28">28 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
